                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "​[b] The most interesting mechanic I've ever seen in an otome. [/b]\n\n​𓊈 [i] PROS [/i] 𓊉\nㅤ — The [u]gameplay[/u]. Having to actually figure out their language to communicate, combined with total freedom to explore the map, makes the experience immersive and engaging.\nㅤ — The [u]characters[/u]. You'll actually want to get to know all of them, which honestly never happens to me in an otome. Unlike other games in the genre where characters often feel like recycled clones with different skins and slang, every single one here feels genuinely distinct to me, with unique identities that you can clearly perceive.\n\n​𓊈 [i] CONS [/i] 𓊉\nㅤ — If you are looking for a story with a [u]deep[/u], [u]thought-provoking[/u] lore or a [u]traditional[/u] romance, this game is not for you. While the interactions between the protagonist and the characters are highly entertaining and some even give off an innocent affection vibe, most routes lack explicit romantic dynamics, and quite a few situationships are left hanging in total ambiguity.\n\n\n​Homicipher is not an average otome where you just pick dialogue choices at specific checkpoints. It forces you to get dragged into its world and gives you a refreshing protagonist who is compelling for the genre.\n\n\n​𓊈 [i] RECOMMENDATIONS [/i] 𓊉\nㅤ — If you expect a [u]standard otome[/u] with lots of dialogues and romantic CGs, this isn't it. The characters mostly communicate in short, simple words, and it's much more about figuring them out.\nㅤ — If you appreciate unconventional yet attractive character designs, are interested in non-cliché protagonists with their own distinct story and value worlds that are worth exploring, I cannot recommend this enough. I relish this game.",
